************* Password Reset High-Level************

Client POST /auth/forgot-password with { "email": "..." }. Server always returns 200 (no user enumeration).
If user exists: generate strong raw token, hash (SHA-256) and store only the hash in MongoDB with expiresAt and used=false. Send the raw token in an email link asynchronously.

User opens front-end reset page with token from query param → front-end POSTs { token, newPassword } to POST /auth/reset-password.

Server atomically finds the token (hash), checks used=false and expiresAt > now, marks it used=true in one DB operation (prevents reuse/race),
updates password (BCrypt), increments
 tokenVersion/updates lastPasswordReset
 to revoke existing JWTs, deletes or
  leaves used token for audit, and
   sends notification email.



🔥 Putting it all together
Full sequence (backend side)

User submits token + new password

Backend hashes the token

Backend atomically finds the token and marks it used

If token invalid/expired/used → reject

Backend loads the user

Backend bcrypt-hashes new password

Backend revokes all active sessions/JWTs

Backend notifies user by email

Backend responds “Password changed successfully”



🎯 Why this whole process is “production level”?

Because it handles:

✔️ Race conditions

(atomic update prevents reuse)

✔️ Secure storage of tokens

(only token hash stored)

✔️ Token expiration (TTL index)
✔️ Single-use tokens

(“used” flag)

✔️ JWT/session revocation

(prevent attackers with old tokens from keeping access)

✔️ User notification

(password change email)

✔️ No user enumeration

(forgot-password always returns success)

✔️ Timing-safe, cryptographically strong token generation
✔️ Protects against DB leaks

(hash only)

These are the same techniques used by:

Google

GitHub

Facebook

AWS

Stripe




When the user submits the reset form with a token + new password,
something like this happens:

POST /reset-password
{
    "token": "<raw token from email>",
    "newPassword": "..."
}


Your backend must:

✔️ Find the token by hashed version

Hash the raw token → match against DB.

✔️ Check these conditions:

Token exists

used == false (has not been used already)

expiresAt > now() (not expired)

✔️ AND mark it used=true in the same MongoDB operation

This is atomic:
find + validate + update = one single database command.

If ANY of those checks fail, the DB returns null.

This prevents:

Token reuse

Race conditions (two fast requests resetting at same time)

Multiple password resets from the same link


✅ 3. After token is used → update the password
Now the password is changed:

user.setPassword(passwordEncoder.encode(newPassword));


This ensures:

bcrypt hashing

salted hashing

no plain text

✅ 4. Revoke all existing sessions / JWTs

This is CRITICAL for real security.
If a user resets their password, any attacker who already had a JWT should instantly lose access.

Production JWT revocation strategies:

Option A — tokenVersion

Store tokenVersion inside the user document

Include it in every JWT during creation

On password reset: user.tokenVersion++

Any past JWT is invalid because the version is old

Option B — lastPasswordChange timestamp

Store lastPasswordChange in user

When validating JWT:

token’s iat must be after lastPasswordChange

If user resets password → update timestamp → invalidate all old tokens

Either option is industry best practice.

✅ 5. Send the user a password-changed notification

Security concept:
If someone resets your password without your knowledge, you must be warned.

So after everything is successful:

We want to alert the user: “Your password has been changed. If you didn’t request this, contact support.”


This protects the user.


*****************************Revoke Tokens*******************************************


When we “revoke tokens,” we mean:

Make all existing login tokens (JWTs, refresh tokens, sessions) invalid so they can no longer be used.

This is important when:

A user resets their password

A user logs out from all devices

A user account is hacked

A refresh token is stolen

Admin forces logout

🟥 Why Do We Need Token Revocation?

A JWT (JSON Web Token):

is self-contained

does NOT need to be stored on the server

can be used until it expires

This creates a problem:

❗ If a hacker steals a JWT, they can keep using it until it expires.

Unless you have token revocation, you cannot stop them.

So you need a way to say:

“Every token created before this time / version is invalid


Method 1: tokenVersion (recommended)

Each user has a field:

tokenVersion: 1


When you create a JWT:

The token contains tokenVersion = 1

When you want to revoke all tokens (like after password reset):
tokenVersion++


Now tokenVersion becomes 2.

What happens?

All old JWTs have version 1 → ❌ INVALID

New JWTs have version 2 → ✔ VALID

The backend checks:

if (jwt.tokenVersion != user.tokenVersion)
    reject token

✔ Fast
✔ Simple
✔ Works perfectly
✔ Zero database lookups needed





🟩 Method 2: lastPasswordChange timestamp

Every JWT includes an “issued at” time (iat).

Each user record stores:

lastPasswordChange: 2025-01-01T12:00:00Z


When validating JWT:

if (jwt.iat < user.lastPasswordChange)
    reject token

When user resets password:
lastPasswordChange = now()


This means:

All old tokens (created before this time) are INVALID

Any hacker with stolen token gets kicked out

New tokens are valid

🟧 Why Token Revocation Is Needed?

Let’s say a hacker steals a user’s JWT.

The JWT is valid for 1 hour.

EVEN if the user resets their password:

Without revocation → hacker can still use that JWT for 1 hour

With revocation → hacker is kicked out immediately

This is why it’s critical.



****************************************Why we should not sign the token JWT using the password***************************
Ah — I see **exactly** where the misunderstanding is happening.

You are correct about one thing:
**JWTs are signed, not encrypted.**

But let’s make this 100% clear because this is where the confusion lies:

---

# ❗✔ **JWT SIGNING ≠ JWT ENCRYPTION**

Signing **protects integrity**, NOT **confidentiality**.

Let me explain:

### ✔ Signing (HMAC or RSA):

* Ensures the token **was not modified**
* Ensures the token was created by the server
* Ensures authenticity

### ❌ Signing does NOT hide the contents.

Anyone can decode the JWT payload **even if it is signed with RSA/private key**.

Repeat:
**Signing NEVER hides data. It only prevents tampering.**

---

# ⭐ **Proof (Do this yourself)**

Take ANY signed JWT (HS256 or RS256) and paste it into:

👉 [https://jwt.io/](https://jwt.io/)

You will ALWAYS be able to see:

* username
* email
* roles
* ANY custom claims

BECAUSE THE PAYLOAD IS NOT ENCRYPTED.

Only the signature (third part of the JWT) uses your private key.

The payload is simply Base64URL encoded — **not encrypted**.

---

# 🟥 Example

A JWT looks like this:

```
header.payload.signature
```

Decode payload:

```
{
  "sub": "john",
  "roles": ["USER"],
  "passwordHash": "$2a$10..."
}
```

Even though RSA is used for signing:

👉 Anyone can read this JSON payload.

They just can’t modify it without breaking the signature.

---

# ❗ WHY YOU CAN'T PUT PASSWORD OR HASH IN JWT

Because:

### 1. **The payload is readable**

Attackers can decode any JWT and see the contents.
Signed ≠ encrypted.

### 2. **Password hash must never be exposed**

Even though bcrypt hashes are slow to crack,
publishing them is a serious security risk.

### 3. **It breaks OWASP and every auth standard**

No secure system puts password hashes inside access tokens.

---

# 🟩 Why private key does NOT protect the payload

When you use RSA signing:

### ✔ Private Key → creates the signature

### ✔ Public Key → verifies the signature

### ❌ Neither of them encrypts the payload

The payload is **NOT encrypted** at all.

It’s just Base64URL encoded — which anyone can decode.

---

# 🟦 *So your statement:*

> “No it cannot read it because it is signed using private key.”

### ❌ Incorrect.

Signing **does not** protect the payload.
Only encryption does.

JWT **Signed (JWS)** → readable
JWT **Encrypted (JWE)** → hidden payload (rarely used)

Most systems use **JWS**, not **JWE**.

---

# ⭐ *Therefore:*

### ❌ You cannot put password hash in JWT

### ❌ You cannot put sensitive data in JWT

### ❌ You cannot use password hash to invalidate tokens

Because JWT payload is readable to anyone.

---

# 🟩 What you CAN do safely:

Use:

### ✔ tokenVersion

or

### ✔ lastPasswordChange

These values are NOT sensitive, but allow revocation.

---

# ⭐ FINAL SUMMARY

| Concept                             | Truth            |
| ----------------------------------- | ---------------- |
| JWT is signed                       | ✔ Yes            |
| JWT payload is encrypted            | ❌ No             |
| Anyone can read JWT payload         | ✔ Yes            |
| Password/password hash safe in JWT  | ❌ Never          |
| Use password hash to revoke JWT     | ❌ Impossible     |
| Use tokenVersion/lastPasswordChange | ✔ Correct method |

